{:edb/db-before-t 808 :edb/db-after-t 809 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534121 50 #inst "2026-10-06T03:32:57.095Z" 13194139534121 true] [17592186114922 1100 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sec x+4=0$ for $0 \\leq x <             2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186114922 1100 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\sec x+4=0$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?" 13194139534121 false] [17592186114922 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sec x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\sec x + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n2\\sec x &= -4 \\\\\n\\sec x &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nlies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} < 0$, the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/edfa3375bc3db6b46d1dac36152c42cd.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= π + θ_{R} \\\\\n&= π + \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{8\\pi^2}{9}\n$$" 13194139534121 false] [17592186114922 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\sec x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x =  \\dfrac{1}{(-2)}=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)= \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$ Since $\\cos{x} < 0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/0e370925faa2a4abc7ac613657ecdf27c2fa0eaa66cff05b077f77a71999c742.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot x_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{8\\pi^2}{9}.\n$$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186114923 1100 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}$ for $-180^\\circ \\leq  x <            180^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186114923 1100 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x={\\sqrt{2}}$ for $-180^\\circ \\leq x < 180^\\circ$, what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$?" 13194139534121 false] [17592186114923 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) = 45^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 45^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/1b44825deb488f131a714655b48634f9.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= 360^{∘} - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 315^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution $x_2$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $360^\\circ$:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 315^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -45^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=45^\\circ,-45^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = 45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025\n$$" 13194139534121 false] [17592186114923 1145 "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $45^\\circ$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 45^\\circ.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 45^\\circ.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c722d4fa2b77011e4df5b086c19bb09943446941484cfaf87559ca39a52183bc.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution $x_2$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $360^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx_2 = 315^\\circ - 360^\\circ = -45^\\circ.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=45^\\circ,-45^\\circ.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 = 45\\cdot (-45)=-2\\,025.\n$$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186114924 1100 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = -1$\n2. $\\sec x = 2$\n3. $\\sec x = \\dfrac 1 3$" 13194139534121 false] [17592186114924 1100 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi?$\n\n- $\\sec x  = -1$\n\n- $\\sec x  = 2$\n\n- $\\sec x  = \\dfrac 1 3$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186114924 1145 "Recall that the range of $\\sec x$ for\n\n$$\n0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$[MATH: \\sec x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1,\\infty).]$\n\nSo, the equation $\\sec x = k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." 13194139534121 false] [17592186114924 1145 "Recall that the range of $\\sec x$ for $0\\leq x \\lt 2\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\sec x \\in (-\\infty, -1] \\cup [1,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\sec x = k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." 13194139534121 true] [17592186114967 1100 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x <                2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139534121 true] [17592186114967 1100 "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x= 3$ for $0 \\leq x < 2\\pi$, what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$, rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139534121 false] [17592186114967 1145 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arccos (\\frac{1}{3}) = 1.230959… \\approx 1.2310\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $\\cos{x} > 0$, the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1565/Source/Images/9ea131571a45a21b7339dc32090c32f8.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - 1.2310 \\\\\n&= 5.0522\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534121 false] [17592186114967 1145 "Recall that $\\sec x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\cos x = \\dfrac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})=1.230\\,959…≈1.231\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1.231\\,0$ lies inside the specified domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 1.231\\,0.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$ Since $\\cos{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 1.231\\,0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/50df7e600a9350f2413da4060293116cccf770df200e55f6534d953d68df42ba.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -1.231\\,0 \\\\  & =5.052\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_2 \\approx 1.231\\,0 \\cdot 5.052\\,2 \\approx 6.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534121 true] [17592186115096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277831 13194139534121 true] [17592186115096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277842 13194139534121 true] [17592186115096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277849 13194139534121 true] [17592186115096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277863 13194139534121 true] [17592186115096 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277899 13194139534121 true] [17592186115097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277837 13194139534121 true] [17592186115097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277856 13194139534121 true] [17592186115097 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277894 13194139534121 true] [17592186115098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277810 13194139534121 true] [17592186115098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277817 13194139534121 true] [17592186115098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277824 13194139534121 true] [17592186115098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277906 13194139534121 true] [17592186115140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277803 13194139534121 true] [17592186115140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277870 13194139534121 true] [17592186115140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277877 13194139534121 true] [17592186115140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277883 13194139534121 true] [17592186115140 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277889 13194139534121 true] [17592186115624 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534121 true] [17592186115624 1100 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0 \\le x < 2π$?\n\n1. $\\sec x = 1$\n2. $\\sec x = 2$\n3. $\\sec x = \\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139534121 false] [17592186115624 1100 "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186115624 1145 "Recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions." 13194139534121 true] [17592186115625 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534121 true] [17592186115625 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x = 2$ for $-180^{∘} \\le x < 180^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?" 13194139534121 false] [17592186115625 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186115625 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={60}^{∘},-{60}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=60\\cdot (-60)=-3\\,600.\n$$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186115626 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534121 true] [17592186115626 1100 "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\sec x = 2$ for $-360^{∘} \\le x < 0^{∘}$, what is the numerical value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?" 13194139534121 false] [17592186115626 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=2$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186115626 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={60}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={60}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/9c8adbb0a1594ae5bacfab7f1f92993c67659839599e10ab46d5cf98896e3ec7.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{60}^{∘} \\\\  & ={300}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $X={300}^{∘}$ is not in the domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution within the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={300}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{60}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-{60}^{∘},-{300}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-60\\cdot (-300)=18\\,000\n$$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277803 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277804 13194139534121 true] [17592186277803 1089 "q-120456" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277803 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534121 true] [17592186277803 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5}=-\\sqrt{5}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277803 1113 #uuid "9fb32491-b445-5fcb-aa6f-323a9d2a0ff4" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277803 1145 "We first simplify the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\sqrt{5} & =-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(-\\sqrt{5})}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})=2.034\\,443…≈2.034\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2.034\\,4$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=2.034\\,4.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=1.107\\,2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/f659a13a5a4c9ae998c33b398b777decc8a5603fd18b5579ddebc897ea3dafca.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +1.107\\,2 \\\\  & =4.248\\,7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2.034\\,4\\cdot 4.248\\,7≈8.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277805 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277806 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277807 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277808 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277809 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1041 "selection" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277808 13194139534121 true] [17592186277804 1110 #uuid "63758ecc-6ec3-5da6-85d9-c4a815a4e3e4" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277805 1034 "8.7" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277805 1111 #uuid "2a615638-b235-5dbb-9f57-dcff1d743fe6" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277806 1034 "6.2" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277806 1111 #uuid "09109bdc-22cb-5df6-9abe-b92b3bf86d8e" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277807 1034 "7.3" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277807 1111 #uuid "418c406c-b628-54c7-bd70-2c29457b412b" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277808 1034 "8.6" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277808 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277808 1111 #uuid "05d3cc85-6bb9-54a3-aaaa-448a64b11ea0" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277809 1034 "9.2" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277809 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277809 1111 #uuid "c4b6a924-ad2f-5164-b6e4-a8598dce1d9f" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277810 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277811 13194139534121 true] [17592186277810 1089 "q-120476" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277810 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534121 true] [1759