$x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139534121 true] [17592186277831 1113 #uuid "efe0ceba-4ecd-5baa-8135-9d9e9b625fba" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277831 1145 "Recall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $1$st quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the $4$th quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/17803dc8d1d3ca2e31586969797b401d5d0a2d958bd6628387b512974076df7a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $x=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277832 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277833 13194139534121 true] [17592186277832 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277834 13194139534121 true] [17592186277832 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534121 true] [17592186277832 1041 "field-1" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277832 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277833 13194139534121 true] [17592186277832 1110 #uuid "9fb897ae-ad55-5d50-9379-b17eb38cebf9" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277833 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277833 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277833 1111 #uuid "c8015c35-1438-5f46-bce0-c04