o has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/3f3f1422eca9190a5e0827da456315ba88b074f0bf5e9aab1bb325d131bf1984.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={330}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-30\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=30\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277838 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277839 13194139534121 true] [17592186277838 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534121 true] [17592186277838 1041 "field-1" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277838 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277839 13194139534121 true] [17592186277838 1110 #uuid "698f3a74-cd06-56b3-9a8a-5066d31165a6" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277839 1034 "-30" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277839 1111 #uuid "0f180271-5813-5675-af96-87a62fd5b4be" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277840 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277841 13194139534121 true] [17592186277840 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534121 true] [17592186277840 1041 "field-2" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277840 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277841 13194139534121 true] [17592186277840 1110 #uuid "c9bc9efc-d6b2-5b95-a4ae-5c4495b26294" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277841 1034 "30" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277841 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277841 1111 #uuid "e10babe8-be76-5d41-a5ba-9f5c9fbb8bf5" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277842 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277843 13194139534121 true] [17592186277842 1089 "q-28902" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277842 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534121 true] [17592186277842 1100 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1=3$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277842 1113 #uuid "8e6aa2e6-3374-55d1-b3c7-5bb059323dce" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277842 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/318eba038feb091d6e3adb80ddf325de6039bb331a7713cf1e2c02171a6388d4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277844 13194139534121 true] [17592186277843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277845 13194139534121 true] [17592186277843 1000 #edb/ref 17592186