\theta }_{R}≈{180}^{∘}-{115.771}^{∘}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈{64.229}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/974fad6534267df035b759df9064ed7dfaff80cb299c41744e8888f248ed1eee.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈{180}^{∘}+{64.229}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{244.229}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=115.8\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=244.2\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277885 13194139534121 true] [17592186277884 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277886 13194139534121 true] [17592186277884 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534121 true] [17592186277884 1041 "field-1" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277884 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277886 13194139534121 true] [17592186277884 1110 #uuid "cac192cd-5660-5cfc-995b-620c521d75d8" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277885 1034 "115.7" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277885 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277885 1111 #uuid "616c1241-11ea-5aee-8dd0-0fdba6656d2c" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277886 1034 "115.8" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277886 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277886 1111 #uuid "4671674d-d300-5dac-8d2a-6c7134757439" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277887 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277888 13194139534121 true] [17592186277887 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534121 true] [17592186277887 1041 "field-2" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277887 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277888 13194139534121 true] [17592186277887 1110 #uuid "aec00fe1-72ae-5414-ba1f-56781b77c547" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277888 1034 "244.2" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277888 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277888 1111 #uuid "1a8a757b-3fc2-5e6a-9e31-629d52bf1e9a" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277889 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277890 13194139534121 true] [17592186277889 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277892 13194139534121 true] [17592186277889 1089 "q-324401" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277889 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534121 true] [17592186277889 1100 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139534121 true] [17592186277889 1113 #uuid "aca43237-2858-5ef6-b0b1-f24a1198a232" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277889 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value of $x,$ rounded to three decimal places:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=arccos\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})≈2.062\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}≈\\pi -2.062≈1.080.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the $3$rd quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}≈1.080.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/c8020c19d17f3e79b825cb4e0cad4471e68ec0e2618a12578115a0fc8c014599.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi +1.080 \\\\  & ≈4.222\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are $x=2.1$ and $x=4.2.$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277890 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277891 13194139534121 true] [17592186277890 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534121 true] [17592186277890 1041 "field-1" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277890 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277891 13194139534121 true] [17592186277890 1110 #uuid "591fbe4f-aafd-5d81-bbe7-8f0c605581f3" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277891 1034 "2.1" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277891 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277891 1111 #uuid "0379519a-f193-50a9-868b-84dd5b4253d1" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277892 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277893 13194139534121 true] [17592186277892 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534121 true] [17592186277892 1041 "field-2" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277892 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277893 13194139534121 true] [17592186277892 1110 #uuid "b6a37fc8-abfa-5d16-a531-194386fd17fb" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277893 1034 "4.2" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277893 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534121 true] [17592186277893 1111 #uuid "8983d381-2a60-5879-b957-a3ac8f7ef730" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277894 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277895 13194139534121 true] [17592186277894 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277897 13194139534121 true] [17592186277894 1089 "q-325125" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277894 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534121 true] [17592186277894 1100 "Find the two solutions to the equation $(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1)$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277894 1113 #uuid "71b4b55b-5a45-5f2c-88ad-262e17c40ef6" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277894 1145 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+\\sqrt{2})\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\,(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=arccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={45}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fourth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977622/assets/cf16066cb8b8368129be39a22f5c15727ac698f44d6f870511c2e07ec317d643.png)\n\nAnother solution $X$ lies in the $4$th quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={315}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-45\\,{\\,}^{∘}$ and $x=45\\,{\\,}^{∘}.$" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277895 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277896 13194139534121 true] [17592186277895 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534121 true] [17592186277895 1041 "field-1" 13194139534121 true] [17592186277895 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277896 13194139534121 true] [17592186277895 1110 #uuid "b4e5aa6f-9a17-54a6-ae93-a67e558f8d79" 13194139534121 